From: ReadyNAS NG <readynasng@build>
Subject: [PATCH 02/27] sparc: Padre: MMU and caches

The Padre MMU is not an SRMMU: it walks a two-level i386-like table
(1024 x 4 MB directory, 1024 x 4 KB tables), has its own fault
registers and ASIs, and maps the kernel and I/O through fixed windows.
padre_mm.c replaces srmmu.c: page tables in the uncached area at the
top of the DDR (the MMU reads memory, the write-back D-cache is not
coherent), TLB and cache flushes, DMA helpers. pgtable/pgalloc get a
Padre variant (folded pmd, PGTABLE_LEVELS 2, no HIGHMEM), user space
is 0-1 GB (TASK_SIZE 0x40000000).

The window overflow/underflow and trap entry stack checks (etrap, rtrap,
wof, wuf) and srmmu_fault use the Padre MMU registers, as the 2.6.17
kernel did; MMU-miss traps go to the fault handler. do_sparc_fault()
ignores the stale write bit on instruction faults and no longer
dereferences a NULL exception entry; padre.trace_fault=N logs the
first N user faults.
